  "body": "## What changed\n\n- `Tool::meridian()` adds the radial shoulder `(flute_len, diameter / 2)` when a flute ends before the tip reaches the full radius.\n- The cone arm (sharp taper, drill) gets the shoulder. This is the case that the issue names.\n- The short-flute branch of `tip_arc` (ball nose, bull nose) gets the same shoulder. The issue's \"Done means\" asks for every tool kind, and this branch has the same gap.\n- The meridian now ends where `Tool::profile` ends. This is the rule that #3929 gave the flat and ball taper tips.\n- The shoulder is added only when it is at least `MIN_LEN` (1e-6 mm). A tool whose flute reaches the full radius keeps the same meridian points, bit for bit.\n- `a_sharp_taper_keeps_its_profile_and_digest` pinned the old short-flute meridian (`Taper 10 mm, 30 deg, flute 5`). Its expected points now include the shoulder. The profile and the `same_shape` checks in that test do not change.\n- Decision record: `docs/decisions/3938-cone-shoulder.md`.\n\n## Cause\n\nThe cone arm pushed `(fl, r)` only when `fl > top`. A short flute has `fl == top`, so the meridian ended at `(fl, fl * tan)`. `Tool::profile` always adds `(zc, r)` at the flute top. `tip_arc` ended a short ball or bull flute on the arc in the same way.\n\n## Who reads the meridian\n\n- No production code calls `Tool::meridian()` today. Only tests in `camcraft-tools` and `camcraft-sim` read it.\n- So no toolpath, posted program or collision verdict changes now. The fix stops a later reader (the sub-issues of #3351) from seeing a smaller tool than the profile.\n- The shoulder is a flat segment. `Meridian::atoms` makes no atom from a flat segment. The solid law pairing `meridian_atoms_match_cutter_atoms_for_each_mill_kind` (sim) stays green with no change.\n\n## Tests (final head 55fda4df5)\n\n- Pin: `meridian::tests::a_short_flute_meridian_has_the_profile_shoulder` (camcraft-tools lib). It covers a sharp taper, a drill, a ball nose, a bull nose, an end mill and a T-slot. It checks that the meridian ends at the profile's flute top, that the cone still ends at the flute top, and that no atom has zero height.\n- Red before the fix: yes. On unchanged source: `left: Some((5.0, 1.3397459621556136)) right: Some((5.0, 5.0))`.\n- Mutation 1: revert the cone guard to `if fl > top`. The pin is red (with `a_sharp_taper_keeps_its_profile_and_digest`). Restore: green.\n- Mutation 2: disable the `tip_arc` shoulder. The pin is red. Restore: green.\n- `cargo xtask gate quick --base upstream/main`: 1 step (`tools suites: lib`), ok.\n- `cargo clippy -p camcraft-tools --all-targets -- -D warnings`: clean.\n- `cargo test -p camcraft-tools --tests`: all suites ok.\n- `cargo test -p camcraft-sim --lib`: 249 passed.\n- Heavy (P1, safety floor, one targeted step): `cargo xtask gate heavy --base upstream/main --only \"app heavy: tool_body\" --only \"app heavy: verify\"`: 2 steps, ok (387 s, 3 s). No heavy test reads `Tool::meridian()`. The reach (105 steps) comes only from the crate dependency, so the whole lane was not run (AGENTS.md \"Heavy lanes at review\").\n- Example catalog: `app suites: example_catalog`, `example_catalog_adaptive`, `example_catalog_edge_work`: 3 steps, ok.\n\n## Goldens moved\n\nNone. The three example catalog suites ran on the final head and passed. No `*.nc`, `*.cl` or `examples/catalog/**` file changed.\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- The bull-nose profile clamps the corner radius to a short flute, so it draws a different arc under the flute top than the meridian. The two agree at the flute top after this fix. The arc below is out of scope.\n- The solid law (`Cutter::atoms`) has no disc at the flute top. A zero-height shoulder has no volume, so it was not changed.\n- The full heavy lanes were not run (see above).\n\n## Overlaps\n\n- #3946 touches `crates/tools/src/meridian.rs` and `meridian_tests.rs`. It changes other lines of `Tool::meridian()` (a form-mill branch and the input check), not the cone arm or `tip_arc`. Whichever PR merges second refreshes.\n\n`crates/tools/src/meridian_tests.rs` is 403 lines (371 before).\n\nChangelog: The tool meridian of a sharp taper, a drill, a ball nose or a bull nose with a short flute now steps out to the full radius at the flute top, as the tool profile does.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nFixes #3938\n",
